WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:07.883
Every cable in your school
goes to one box in a cupboard.

2
00:00:08.103 --> 00:00:12.375
That box is a switch. It joins
them all up into one network.

3
00:00:12.875 --> 00:00:15.755
A school network is
hardware you can point at.

4
00:00:15.975 --> 00:00:19.504
The switch is the box every
cable in the building comes to.

5
00:00:19.724 --> 00:00:24.647
The server is a computer with no
screen. It stores everyone's files.

6
00:00:24.867 --> 00:00:29.046
An access point sits on the
ceiling. The wi-fi comes from there.

7
00:00:29.266 --> 00:00:33.910
Laptops, tablets, the printer and
the whiteboard all join through them.

8
00:00:34.130 --> 00:00:37.706
At home you have a router.
It does the switch's job.

9
00:00:38.206 --> 00:00:41.550
Look around your classroom and
count what is on the network.

10
00:00:41.770 --> 00:00:46.042
The computers, of course. And the
interactive smartboard at the front.

11
00:00:46.262 --> 00:00:52.253
That is the big screen the teacher writes
on. It is on the network like any computer.

12
00:00:52.473 --> 00:00:57.953
By the door is the multifunction device.
One machine prints, scans and copies.

13
00:00:58.173 --> 00:01:01.981
Everybody shares it, instead of
three machines in three rooms.

14
00:01:02.481 --> 00:01:07.961
Being on the network gives you services:
things you can do because you are connected.

15
00:01:08.181 --> 00:01:13.800
Digital files. Save your work on the
server and open it on any device in school.

16
00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:19.222
Printed documents. Press print on a
tablet, and the school printer prints it.

17
00:01:19.442 --> 00:01:24.086
The World Wide Web. Pages stored
on computers all over the world.

18
00:01:24.306 --> 00:01:29.414
Email and video calls too: messages
and faces across the network.

19
00:01:29.914 --> 00:01:33.397
A network is mostly good.
Think what it gives you.

20
00:01:33.617 --> 00:01:37.286
Everyone can share files.
Everyone can share one printer.

21
00:01:37.506 --> 00:01:41.174
Every laptop and tablet can
reach the World Wide Web.

22
00:01:41.394 --> 00:01:44.924
Two classes can work on one
document at the same time.

23
00:01:45.144 --> 00:01:50.624
It keeps a backup: a second copy,
so a broken computer loses nothing.

24
00:01:51.124 --> 00:01:55.443
A network costs you things too.
It is good to know both sides.

25
00:01:55.663 --> 00:02:00.028
When the network is down, nobody
can save and nobody can print.

26
00:02:00.248 --> 00:02:04.938
Cables, switches and servers cost
money. Somebody has to look after them.

27
00:02:05.158 --> 00:02:08.827
A file you share can be seen
by people you did not mean.

28
00:02:09.047 --> 00:02:14.016
It can share a computer virus: a program
that copies itself and does harm.

29
00:02:14.236 --> 00:02:18.184
When it stops, everybody stops
at once, not just one person.

30
00:02:18.684 --> 00:02:22.817
A network is hardware: a switch,
a server and access points.

31
00:02:23.037 --> 00:02:28.377
Being on it gives you services: files,
printing and the World Wide Web.

32
00:02:28.597 --> 00:02:34.124
It has advantages and disadvantages.
Knowing both is understanding a network.
